                    <li id="cf-fn-17">For example, Canada tightened loan-to-value and debt-to income limits on mortgage lending over the 2009 to 2012 period. See Ivo Krznar and James Morsink,&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://econpapers.repec.org/paper/imfimfwpa/14_2f83.htm" class="offsite" target="_blank">&ldquo;With Great Power Comes Great Responsibility: Macroprudential Tools at Work in Canada,&rdquo;</a>&nbsp;IMF Working Paper WP/14/83, May 2014.<br />
Beginning in 2010, Israel also implemented a package of macroprudential tools to restrict the supply of housing credit. See Stanley Fischer,&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://ideas.repec.org/h/mtp/chapts/0262027348-07.html" class="offsite" target="_blank">&ldquo;Macroprudential Policy in Action: Israel,&rdquo;</a>&nbsp;in George A. Akerlof, Olivier J. Blanchard, David Romer, and Joseph E. Stiglitz, eds.,&nbsp;<em>What Have We Learned? Macroeconomic Policy After the Crisis</em>, Cambridge, Mass: The MIT Press, 2014, pp. 87-98.<br />
Spain introduced dynamic loan-loss provisioning in 2000. This method builds up reserves during good economic times according to the historical losses experienced by the asset classes held in the bank&rsquo;s portfolio. This buffer is then available to absorb losses in bad times. See Eliana Balla and Andrew McKenna,&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://ideas.repec.org/a/fip/fedreq/y2009ifallp383-418nv.95no.4.html" class="offsite" target="_blank">&ldquo;Dynamic Provisioning: A Countercyclical Tool for Loan Loss Reserves,&rdquo;</a>&nbsp;Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond&nbsp;<em>Economic Quarterly</em>&nbsp;95, Fall 2009, pp. 383-418.
